>> title: Tamagoyaki
>> category: Eggs
>> tags: japanese, egg, dashi, side, bento, quick
>> counters: Japanese Home Cooking
>> aka: 卵焼き, tamagoyaki, tamagoyaki, たまごやき, 玉子焼き, だし巻き卵, dashimaki tamago, rolled omelette, Japanese rolled egg
>> pairs-with: dashi, gohan, shogayaki
>> servings: 4
>> time: 20 min
>> slack: unforgiving — a pan hot enough to brown the first layer browns every layer after it, and the stripes show through the finished roll

>> step: This is the だし巻き — the dashi version, savoury and loose. The sweet Kantō one is a different recipe.
This is the だし巻き, the dashi version — savoury, loose enough to wobble, eaten warm or cold out of a lunchbox.

>> step: whisk and strain
Whisk @eggs{4} with @dashi{4%Tbs}(60 mL), @usukuchi soy sauce{1%tsp}(5 mL), @mirin{1%tsp}(5 mL), @sugar{1%tsp} and @fine sea salt{1%pinch} in a #bowl{}, then pour it through a sieve.

>> step: pour the first third, set 40 sec, roll
Wipe a #tamagoyaki pan{} with @neutral oil{2%tsp}(10 mL) over medium-low heat, pour a third of @&(~1)egg{} in, ~set{40%sec} until the top is barely wet, then roll it away from you.

>> step: roll two more thirds over the top
Pour half of what is left down the pan and lift @&(~1)roll{} so it runs underneath, ~set{40%sec}, roll it back over the top, and do the same with the last of the egg.

>> step: shape in the mat, rest 5 min
Turn @&(~1)tamagoyaki{} onto a #bamboo mat{}, roll it up and squeeze it gently square, and let it ~rest{5%min} before cutting it into eight.
